theory: [
{q:'Compare EAS Cloud builds (Step 8A) with Local builds (Step 8B) across the six tutorial criteria, and explain Continuous Native Generation (CNG) and release keystore signing.',pts:['6 criteria: setup, speed, internet, computer specs (8/16GB RAM), iPhone builds (Windows/Linux vs Mac+Xcode), control','EAS commands (eas login, eas build:configure, eas build --platform android --profile preview/production)','Local build steps (Android Studio, JDK 17, ANDROID_HOME, adb devices, npx expo run:android, ./gradlew assembleRelease vs bundleRelease)','Continuous Native Generation (CNG): android/ and ios/ generated from app.json, wiped by prebuild --clean','Release signing warning: debug key vs release keystore backup + store requirements ($25 + 2-week closed test vs $99/yr)'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> Expo Go is only for development previews. To ship a real installable mobile app, an Expo developer must compile native binaries either in Expo's cloud (EAS, Step 8A) or locally on their own machine (Step 8B).</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> the tutorial compares Cloud (EAS) and Local builds across six criteria: (1) <b>Setup:</b> EAS needs almost none (<code>npm install -g eas-cli</code>, <code>eas login</code>, <code>eas build:configure</code>), whereas local requires several GB of Android Studio, Android SDK tools, <b>JDK 17</b> and <code>ANDROID_HOME</code> / <code>platform-tools</code> environment variables; (2) <b>Speed:</b> free-tier EAS builds can wait in a cloud queue, whereas local builds have no queue (though the first Gradle download takes 10–30 minutes); (3) <b>Internet:</b> EAS needs internet throughout, while local needs it mainly for initial setup; (4) <b>Computer needed:</b> EAS runs from any laptop, while local requires at least <b>8 GB RAM (16 GB recommended)</b>; (5) <b>iPhone builds:</b> EAS can build iOS from Windows or Linux, whereas local iOS builds require a <b>Mac with Xcode</b>; (6) <b>Control:</b> local gives full direct access to native tooling (<code>./gradlew assembleRelease</code> for APK, <code>./gradlew bundleRelease</code> for Play Store AAB). Under <b>Continuous Native Generation (CNG)</b>, Expo generates <code>android/</code> and <code>ios/</code> from <code>app.json</code>; running <code>npx expo prebuild --clean</code> wipes hand-edits, so native config belongs in <code>app.json</code>. Finally, local builds default to a <b>debug key</b>; Play Store uploads require a backed-up <b>release keystore</b> (losing it permanently blocks future updates to that listing).</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> a student on a 4 GB Windows laptop uses <code>eas build --platform android --profile preview</code> to generate a test APK in the cloud and later switches to <code>--profile production</code> to produce a signed AAB for Google Play's 2-week closed test.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> Expo Router and Fast Refresh speed up daily coding, CNG keeps native folders reproducible from <code>app.json</code>, and EAS or local Gradle turns the project into signed APK/AAB artifacts.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> pick EAS Cloud when hardware is weak, internet is steady or building iOS from Windows; pick local Gradle builds when you have a 16 GB machine and want zero queue times.</p>`}
]
