theory: [
{q:'Contrast web and mobile deployment, list six deployment best practices and pitfalls, and describe the complete Osisi production stack from the lectures.',pts:['Web vs Mobile deployment (instant server update & low cost vs store review, versioning, device fragmentation, high cost)','6 best practices: Git, env vars + dev/staging/prod, CI/CD automation, monitoring/alerts, rollback strategy, security (HTTPS, CORS, access controls)','6 common pitfalls: works on my machine, manual drift, no rollback, no monitoring, skipping staging, mobile store/fragmentation bugs','Osisi stack: Next.js on Vercel, Convex backend, Cloudflare images, Resend emails, Paystack payments, Auth0 authentication'],model:`<p><b>Background:</b> deployment bridges the gap between local code and real users on real devices. A poor deployment pipeline causes downtime, security breaches and lost user trust.</p><p><b>Answer and solution:</b> web and mobile deployment differ sharply: <b>web apps</b> update instantaneously on the server at an immediate URL with low distribution cost, whereas <b>mobile apps</b> face app-store gatekeepers (simple Android review vs stricter, slower iOS review), version fragmentation across devices, offline constraints and user-initiated updates. Six best practices prevent common pitfalls: (1) use <b>Git version control</b>; (2) separate dev, staging and production configs via <b>environment variables</b> (avoiding the <i>"works on my machine"</i> pitfall); (3) automate builds and deploys with <b>CI/CD</b> (avoiding manual drift and human error); (4) test in <b>staging</b> before production; (5) maintain a <b>rollback/hotfix strategy</b> and live <b>monitoring/alerts</b>; and (6) enforce <b>HTTPS, CORS and access controls</b>. In the lecturer's real-world case study, <b>Osisi</b> combines specialized managed platforms: <b>Next.js (React)</b> deployed on <b>Vercel</b> for the frontend, <b>Convex</b> for the reactive backend and database, <b>Cloudflare</b> for static image assets, <b>Resend</b> for transactional emails, <b>Paystack</b> for payments and <b>Auth0</b> for authentication.</p><p><b>Scenario:</b> when an Osisi engineer pushes a family-tree update to GitHub, Vercel builds a staging preview, Convex validates the schema, and monitoring alerts watch error rates so the team can roll back in one click if a regression appears.</p><p><b>How they combine:</b> managed services (Vercel, Convex, Cloudflare, Auth0, Resend, Paystack) remove raw server maintenance, while CI/CD, staging environments and rollback plans keep releases safe.</p><p><b>Conclusion:</b> choose tools that fit the project scope without over-engineering, automate every release step, and treat deployment as an ongoing loop of monitoring and iteration.</p>`}
]
